      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1374147047,
      "text": "Dysphania ambrosioides, formerly Chenopodium ambrosioides, known as epazote, Jesuit's tea, Mexican tea or wormseed, is an annual or short-lived perennial herb native to the Americas."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "toxicity": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Dysphania ambrosioides」の節「Toxicity」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dysphania_ambrosioides#Toxicity",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1378848170,
        "heading": "Toxicity",
        "text": "Humans have died from overdoses of D. ambrosioides essential oils (attributed to the ascaridole content). Symptoms include severe gastroenteritis with pain, vomiting, and diarrhea, dizziness, headache, temporary deafness, kidney and liver damage, convulsions, paralysis, death.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
